1
00:00:00,525 --> 00:00:03,685
Two neighboring body cells look almost identical.

2
00:00:03,685 --> 00:00:04,905
One is healthy.

3
00:00:04,905 --> 00:00:06,965
The other is making viruses.

4
00:00:06,965 --> 00:00:08,525
How can your immune system tell them

5
00:00:08,525 --> 00:00:10,745
apart without destroying both?

6
00:00:10,745 --> 00:00:13,345
T cells help solve that problem by reading

7
00:00:13,345 --> 00:00:15,085
tiny molecular clues.

8
00:00:15,085 --> 00:00:17,915
Their story is about recognition, teamwork, and

9
00:00:17,915 --> 00:00:19,203
knowing when to stop.

10
00:00:21,715 --> 00:00:23,775
T cells are white blood cells in the

11
00:00:23,775 --> 00:00:27,155
adaptive immune system, alongside B cells.

12
00:00:27,155 --> 00:00:30,065
This system builds highly targeted responses and

13
00:00:30,065 --> 00:00:32,475
can retain a memory of encounters.

14
00:00:32,475 --> 00:00:34,155
But it never works alone.

15
00:00:34,155 --> 00:00:36,905
Barriers and innate immune cells provide early

16
00:00:36,905 --> 00:00:40,315
defenses and signals that help guide the response.

17
00:00:40,315 --> 00:00:43,040
Think coordinated team, not lone warrior.

18
00:00:45,615 --> 00:00:49,115
T cells develop from stem cells in bone marrow.

19
00:00:49,115 --> 00:00:51,565
Their precursors travel to the thymus, an

20
00:00:51,565 --> 00:00:53,515
organ in the upper chest.

21
00:00:53,515 --> 00:00:56,195
There, selection preserves cells able to read

22
00:00:56,195 --> 00:00:59,040
the body's molecular displays and removes many

23
00:00:59,075 --> 00:01:01,495
that react too strongly to self.

24
00:01:01,495 --> 00:01:05,240
These checks reduce autoimmunity risk, but don't eliminate it.

25
00:01:07,748 --> 00:01:10,188
Most T cells recognize an antigen as a

26
00:01:10,188 --> 00:01:13,228
small protein fragment, called a peptide.

27
00:01:13,228 --> 00:01:17,013
Another cell displays it on a protein called MHC.

28
00:01:17,068 --> 00:01:20,038
A T cell receptor recognizes a particular peptide

29
00:01:20,038 --> 00:01:24,628
and MHC combination, not a whole virus floating freely.

30
00:01:24,628 --> 00:01:26,828
Think of a sample on a display stand.

31
00:01:26,828 --> 00:01:28,833
Both pieces matter for recognition.

32
00:01:31,445 --> 00:01:34,085
Dendritic cells collect material in tissues and

33
00:01:34,085 --> 00:01:36,165
carry it to nearby lymph nodes, where

34
00:01:36,165 --> 00:01:38,005
T cells circulate.

35
00:01:38,005 --> 00:01:41,405
For an inexperienced T cell, recognizing its matching

36
00:01:41,405 --> 00:01:44,485
peptide display usually isn't enough.

37
00:01:44,485 --> 00:01:48,025
An activated dendritic cell also provides co-stimulation,

38
00:01:48,025 --> 00:01:51,300
an additional signal that helps start the response.

39
00:01:51,475 --> 00:01:54,340
Other chemical signals help shape what happens next.

40
00:01:56,882 --> 00:02:00,422
Once activated, the responding T cell divides.

41
00:02:00,422 --> 00:02:03,462
Its descendants share the same receptor specificity,

42
00:02:03,462 --> 00:02:05,742
building a larger group able to recognize

43
00:02:05,742 --> 00:02:07,442
that molecular target.

44
00:02:07,442 --> 00:02:09,622
This is clonal expansion.

45
00:02:09,622 --> 00:02:12,212
Some descendants become working cells that help

46
00:02:12,212 --> 00:02:15,742
coordinate defenses or attack infected cells.

47
00:02:15,742 --> 00:02:18,837
The immune system expands selected responders.

48
00:02:18,862 --> 00:02:21,977
It doesn't turn every T cell into a universal fighter.

49
00:02:24,625 --> 00:02:28,035
CD4 helper T cells generally recognize peptides on

50
00:02:28,035 --> 00:02:32,480
MHC class II displayed by antigen-presenting cells.

51
00:02:32,505 --> 00:02:35,265
They coordinate other cells through direct contact

52
00:02:35,265 --> 00:02:38,160
and chemical messengers, called cytokines.

53
00:02:38,185 --> 00:02:41,720
Some help B cells develop into antibody-producing cells.

54
00:02:41,745 --> 00:02:45,170
Others help microbe-eating cells destroy what they've swallowed.

55
00:02:45,225 --> 00:02:46,615
The distinction matters.

56
00:02:46,615 --> 00:02:48,800
B cells make antibodies.

57
00:02:48,825 --> 00:02:50,460
Helper T cells provide support.

58
00:02:53,158 --> 00:02:57,658
CD8 cytotoxic or killer, T cells recognize peptides

59
00:02:57,658 --> 00:03:00,263
on MHC class 1, found on most

60
00:03:00,308 --> 00:03:02,193
cells with a nucleus.

61
00:03:02,238 --> 00:03:04,338
When an activated killer meets an infected

62
00:03:04,338 --> 00:03:06,773
cell displaying a matching peptide, it can

63
00:03:06,798 --> 00:03:11,053
trigger apoptosis: the cell's controlled self-destruction.

64
00:03:11,078 --> 00:03:13,128
It does not swallow the virus.

65
00:03:13,128 --> 00:03:15,023
It removes a cellular home where the

66
00:03:15,078 --> 00:03:16,493
virus is reproducing.

67
00:03:19,025 --> 00:03:22,805
A defense powerful enough to kill cells needs restraint.

68
00:03:22,805 --> 00:03:26,585
Regulatory T cells help suppress excessive responses and

69
00:03:26,585 --> 00:03:29,205
limit attacks on the body's own tissues.

70
00:03:29,205 --> 00:03:30,755
They're part of a wider set of

71
00:03:30,755 --> 00:03:34,125
immune checks, not an infallible safety switch.

72
00:03:34,125 --> 00:03:37,325
Protection means striking a balance, enough action

73
00:03:37,325 --> 00:03:40,215
to control a threat without unnecessary damage

74
00:03:40,215 --> 00:03:41,393
to healthy tissue.

75
00:03:43,892 --> 00:03:46,352
When an infection subsides, most of the

76
00:03:46,352 --> 00:03:48,172
expanded T cells die.

77
00:03:48,172 --> 00:03:50,982
Memory cells remain, ready to respond faster

78
00:03:50,982 --> 00:03:52,572
to a matching target.

79
00:03:52,572 --> 00:03:55,972
Vaccines often engage both T cells and B cells

80
00:03:55,972 --> 00:03:59,407
to build this kind of protection. Responses vary.

81
00:03:59,452 --> 00:04:02,512
Memory can reduce severe disease without preventing

82
00:04:02,512 --> 00:04:05,791
every infection, and protection isn't always lifelong.

83
00:04:08,248 --> 00:04:10,808
HIV shows why T cells matter.

84
00:04:10,808 --> 00:04:14,968
It damages CD4 T cells and weakens immune defenses.

85
00:04:14,968 --> 00:04:18,723
Cancer medicine sometimes harnesses T cells instead.

86
00:04:18,728 --> 00:04:20,868
Some treatments release immune brakes.

87
00:04:20,868 --> 00:04:24,088
Others engineer T cells to recognize cancer.

88
00:04:24,088 --> 00:04:26,728
These approaches can help selected patients, but

89
00:04:26,728 --> 00:04:28,638
don't work for everyone and can cause

90
00:04:28,638 --> 00:04:30,288
serious side effects.

91
00:04:30,288 --> 00:04:31,586
They're not cure-alls.

92
00:04:34,058 --> 00:04:35,588
Back to our two cells.

93
00:04:35,588 --> 00:04:38,498
An activated killer T cell recognizes a matching

94
00:04:38,498 --> 00:04:40,358
viral peptide display.

95
00:04:40,358 --> 00:04:41,348
What can it do?

96
00:04:41,348 --> 00:04:44,328
Make antibodies, swallow viruses, or signal the

97
00:04:44,328 --> 00:04:45,873
infected cell to die.

98
00:04:47,798 --> 00:04:50,178
Signal the infected cell to die.

99
00:04:50,178 --> 00:04:53,458
Across their different roles, T cells recognize, help,

100
00:04:53,458 --> 00:04:55,818
kill, regulate, and remember.

101
00:04:55,818 --> 00:04:58,042
Effective defense depends on teamwork.
